  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 42a7ef911f4c60e408989a759d2b9b18, type: 3}
  m_Name: PHP_ARRAYS
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::CodeEscape.Programming.LessonData
  id: PHP_ARRAYS
  language: 2
  conceptTitle: Arrays
  symbol: '[ ]'
  relatedQuestionIds:
  - PHP_09_ARRAYS
  - HZ_PHP_05_ARRAYS
  topics:
  - Arrays
  definition: An array stores several values, in order, inside one variable.
  howItWorks: 'Create an array with square brackets and commas: $list = ["a", "b"];
    (array("a", "b") also works). Each item has an index that starts at 0, so $list[0]
    is the first item. count($list) gives the number of items.'
  exampleCode: '<?php

    $snacks = ["apple", "chips", "grapes", "nuts"];

    echo
    $snacks[1];

    echo count($snacks);

    ?>'
  exampleExplanation: '$snacks[1] is the second item, chips, because counting starts
    at 0. count($snacks) returns how many items there are: 4.'
  tip: Like every PHP variable, an array's name starts with $.
